<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dreyfus Affair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1894">1894</a>–<a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dreyfus Affair was a political scandal that divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1894 to 1906. Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus">Alfred Dreyfus</a>, a Jewish army officer, was wrongly convicted of spying for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and the army covered up evidence pointing to Major Ferdinand Esterhazy. Émile Zola's open letter J'Accuse in 1898 turned it into a national conflict between republicans and their nationalist, Catholic and antisemitic opponents, and Dreyfus was finally cleared in 1906.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dreyfus-affair.jpg" alt="Dreyfus Affair" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aron Gerschel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfred_Dreyfus_(1859-1935)_-_photo_originale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After defeat by Germany in 1871 the return of Alsace-Lorraine drove French revanchism for a quarter century, and in the <b>Dreyfus Affair</b> of 1894–1906 conservative Catholic nationalists treated a Jewish officer as no true Frenchman.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>His innocence, and the cover-up, split France in the <b>Dreyfus Affair</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-22">22 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Dreyfus Affair</b> divided the nation.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-third-republic">French Third Republic</a></li></ul></section>
